{
  "proposal": "AUDIT (code read, nothing executed). (a) compression._verify_certificate is sound: blocks come from every original clause, each used form is confined to its block support, exhaustive local equality gives F(x)=Q(pi(x)) globally, and entrywise P*L=I gives surjectivity. (b) The skip all_variables_in_odd_blocks is exact: odd |Z| forces a trivial stabiliser, so every unit form is a candidate and rank n follows. (c) Discovery and checking enumerate 2^w points per block whatever its clause count, so cost is polynomial in encoded bits only through the cap w<=8. (d) step() caches on the uncanonicalised restricted tuple and calls refute before elim_plus. Calling refute(reduced) only after elim_plus returns STUCK and non-binary leaves sat, peak and layers identical (refute is UNSAT-only, elim exact) and never adds calls. (e) incompatible_greedy_basis conflates greedy failure with nonexistence.\n\nFIXTURE M9: all 126 positive and 126 negative 5-clauses on 9 variables (no five equal). Hand-derived v5 predictions: compression skipped incompatible_greedy_basis (greedy takes x1 xor xj, j=2..9; a block avoiding variable 1 gets span {0}); core UNKNOWN no_clauses_removed; root IALH 126 windows, 4032 tuples, every table the 20 weight-2/3 tuples with integer hull Z^5, 0 constraints, UNKNOWN; then size-nonincreasing DP refutes with clause counts 252,182,112,62,32,16,8,4 (accepted_dp 8); peak 0, one counting call, zero refutations. So the whole IALH call is waste that gate (d) removes. Lemma (counting, proved here): M9 has no adapted basis at all. Its 8 basis vectors each lie in at most 35 block annihilators, and 280 < 126*4 = 504, yet the common kernel <1...1> is nontrivial. Adapted-basis existence is therefore strictly weaker than exact kernel quotienting. Repair inside already-checked semantics: let a block use forms supported on S u U and test all 2^|S u U| points (same local-to-global lemma, enlarged support); for M9 |S u U| <= 6.\n\nMAIN: NEON58 IS MONOTONE DUALITY. A CNF is sign-consistent if some renaming makes every clause all-positive or all-negative; this is a GF(2) system on co-occurring literals, solved by union-find in linear time. Call it CIM if moreover every positive clause shares a variable with every negative clause (O(|P||T|w) to test). The Neon58 quotient is CIM: P={A_i u B_j}, T=Tr(A) u Tr(B)=Tr(P).\nLemma 1 (hand-checked): restriction, unit propagation and subsumption preserve CIM and never increase |P| or |T|.\nLemma 2 (Eiter-Gottlob IMSAT; rederived): a subsumption-free CIM formula is UNSAT iff T=Tr(P). CIM-SAT is co-DUAL.\nLemma 3 (any CNF): if sum_C 2^-|C| < 1, conditional expectations give a model in O(L*n) bit operations; so every UNSAT state has a clause of width <= log2 m.\nLemma 4 (Fredman-Khachiyan A as DPLL): in a CIM state with both signs present and no Lemma-3 model, take a shortest clause C. Cross-intersection gives x in C occurring in >= |opposite|/log2 m opposite-sign clauses. Branching on x sends volume v=|P||T| to <= (1-1/log2 m)v and <= v-1. Hence R(v) <= 1+R((1-eps)v)+R(v-1): at most m^O(log^2 m) nodes, each O(L*w) with w-bit dyadics. Total encoded-bit work is 2^O(log^3 L), quasi-polynomial, covering detection, search and certificate. The certificate is the branch/UP tree, replayed in nodes*L.\n\nThis replaces the forced ascending quotient order by a proof-backed state-local choice. Independent-test prediction (unimplemented): on the 16-variable Neon58 quotient, breaking ties toward the partner variable, T(i)=2T(i-1)+33 with T(0)=1 gives 511 nodes unmemoised and about a dozen distinct canonical states memoised; k pairs give about m^2/2.\n\nARBITRARY EXTRAS: UNSAT-only, under the deployed-core contract. Any clause subfamily that is CIM and FK-refuted refutes the input; the subset gate is unchanged; SAT/UNKNOWN decides nothing.",
  "missing_theorem": "T-CIM-P (open; equivalent to DUAL in P, open since 1996): a polynomial bounding total work on all CIM inputs. Consequence: any polynomial residual-work invariant claimed for this solver must already hold on CIM, the hereditary class containing the Neon58 quotient, so it would settle that problem. Current knowledge supports only m^o(log m) (FK-B).\nT-DEFECT (exact gap to arbitrary CNF): dual-rail variables make every CNF sign-consistent but leave delta>0 disjoint opposite-sign clause pairs. Lemma 4 needs cross-intersection at every descendant. With delta_C disjoint partners the frequency bound is (|opposite|-delta_C)/log2 m, which is not hereditary because restriction can delete intersecting partners and keep disjoint ones. Needed: a hereditary potential Phi and eps>=1/polylog such that every UNSAT state has a variable with drops (1-eps)Phi and Phi-1. For all CNF this would give quasi-polynomial SAT and contradict ETH, so the theorem must charge delta; the correct charge is unknown. Proved fallback only: a strong backdoor B into CIM gives 2^|B| m^O(log^2 m); finding B is not claimed polynomial.\nT-EXT: for extended-support kernel certificates, bound |S u U| and quotient clause count by encoded input size, or exhibit a family where both blow up.",
  "self_attack": "1. CIM is one restricted class with a quasi-polynomial, not polynomial, bound; nothing here advances general SAT. Its value is calibration: Neon58's closure is co-DUAL.\n2. FK-DPLL certificates are tree-like resolution. Pigeonhole is sign-consistent but not cross-intersecting (pigeon clause i misses hole clauses avoiding i), and Haken's bound rules out any branching-only repair; the h=5 timeout stands. Gate (d) and canonical caching remove waste, not growth.\n3. A good static order already gives peak 2 on the Neon58 quotient. FK bounds tree nodes, not merged layers. Per-state choice breaks layer semantics, so the right measure becomes distinct memoised states, for which I prove nothing beyond the node bound.\n4. Leaving the class: signed-SCC substitution (a equivalent to not-b from (a,b),(-a,-b)) and DP resolvents create mixed-sign clauses. FK mode must use canon only; interleaving with canon_plus/elim loses Lemma 1.\n5. M9 predictions are hand simulation. They assume SymPy returns the identity HNF for Z^5 and that my DP resolvent counts are right; a missed subsumption changes accepted_dp, not sat. One root call is 4032 UP runs over 252 clauses, plausibly seconds.\n6. Extended-support quotient blocks can need 2^|S u U| clauses. M9 is benign only because its kernel is one vector with a star basis. The idea may overlap earlier section/orbit work I cannot see.\n7. CIM-core selection is biclique-like (NP-hard to maximise). Greedy can miss the 48-clause core under adversarial positive extras meeting most negatives.\n8. The 511-node prediction depends on my tie rule; only m^O(log^2 m) is proved.\n9. Lemma 2 and the FK bound are cited from memory and rederived by hand, not Lean-checked.",
  "mechanism_signature": "CIM-FK: a GF(2) sign-consistency test plus a cross-intersection test identify monotone-duality residuals (the Neon58 quotient is one). State-local Fredman-Khachiyan branching (shortest clause, most frequent opposite-sign variable) with volume potential |P||T| gives a quasi-polynomial node bound and a replayable tree certificate. UNSAT-only CIM-core mode handles extras. Audit by-products: a trajectory-identical elim-first IALH gate, and proof that M9 has a kernel but no adapted basis.",
  "delta_from_prior": "UD and link-twin contraction (00073/00077) rewrite product or twin structure and need new checked transformations; they do nothing for non-product dual pairs such as threshold M9. CIM-FK needs no rewriting, kernel, twins or product, only branching, so soundness is plain DPLL and the bound is a published theorem restated with a hand-checked heredity lemma. New negative result: polynomial control of Neon58's hereditary closure is equivalent to DUAL in P. New audit facts: the odd-block skip is exact, M9 provably has no adapted basis despite a kernel, and the IALH gate is trajectory-identical. Not re-proposed: T94 semantics, ATOM-REFINE, orbit covers.",
